/-!
# Original higher-field exclusion and component sieve

Every higher-field alternative in the actual component catalogue is excluded.
The source Borel, torus and inverter marks, literal quotient actions, and
relative component normality are retained throughout. The external PSL₂ and
odd PSL₃ routes work on their proved moved spaces and retain a possible
component-central ambient complement. The derived symplectic and G₂ rows,
projective SL₃(4), and special and projective unitary rows are excluded by
their native source arguments.

For PSL₂(4), containment and the five-point marking yield the terminal
quadraticity contradiction on V_H/U_H. For full SL₃(4), the scalar cubic is
fixed-free and the original source gives U_H=[U_H,K]. Native recognition,
no-FF saturation and homogeneity construct a natural constituent whose full
orbit is its direct sum with the original graph translate. These actual
natural-plus-dual coordinates contradict the common-line critical geometry.
The full scalar center is preserved through this argument.

Combining the higher-field exclusion with the checked cover sieve proves
L₁≤K, the remaining quotient list, H=KT and U_H=[U_H,K], also for components
of unrestricted original overgroups. The component quotient PSL₃(2) remains
in the list. Exclusion of the whole quotient H/O₂(H)≃PSL₃(2), the additional
binary automorphism clause, and the complete 13.8.19–21 assembly remain with
qt-d-138-fields-source. The linear-consumer adapters retain that one binary
exclusion explicitly. Earlier source premises and the component producer
retain their original owners through qt-d-1381-source.

Source: Aschbacher–Smith, 13.8.20–21, printed pp.954–957.
-/
